PART 5: THE INDIVIDUAL LESSON EXPERIENCE

This is one of the most important parts of the entire project.

Every concept must have its own comprehensive educational page.

Example URL:

/education/lesson/fair-value-gap

Required lesson structure

1. Lesson Header

* Category
* Concept name
* Short definition
* Difficulty level
* Reading time
* Lesson number
* Breadcrumb navigation

2. Introduction

Explain what the concept is and why it is studied.

3. Fundamental Explanation

Break the concept down from first principles.

Assume the reader has no prior knowledge.

4. Visual Explanation

Create an original annotated price chart.

Use:

* Candlesticks
* Swing points
* Labels
* Price levels
* Highlighted zones
* Arrows
* Explanatory annotations

All illustrations must be original and relevant to the specific lesson.

5. Concept Anatomy

Explain the different components of the concept.

6. Identification

Explain the visual characteristics used to recognize the concept in historical or hypothetical charts.

7. Context

Explain how the concept relates to broader market structure and other terminology.

8. Examples

Include clearly labeled hypothetical bullish and bearish chart examples where relevant.

9. Misconceptions

Explain what the concept does not establish and common interpretation errors.

10. Knowledge Check

Add three to five multiple-choice questions.

Provide instant explanations after answers.

11. Frequently Asked Questions

Include three to six relevant questions.

12. Key Takeaways

Provide a concise summary.

13. Related Concepts

Connect the lesson to relevant glossary terms and other articles.

14. Continue Learning

Include:

* Previous lesson
* Next lesson
* Related module
* Return to curriculum

Make every lesson feel like a properly designed educational article, not a generic blog post.

